by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-03T08:44:26Z

Thanks Amoifr, the tests, the readonly class and the compiler pass all landed well. Rebased on 8.2, with four changes before merging.

The sub-request fix had to be undone, and that one is my fault: my earlier point was about cosmetic repetition, and taking it literally inverted the bug. Clearing `$this->deauthentication` on the first `collect()` does not skip sub-request profiles, it skips whichever profile is collected first, and that is a sub-request, since their `kernel.response` fires inside the main controller. Running a real `Profiler` with one embedded sub-request:

```
with the clearing:     /main deauthentication=NULL      /sub  deauthentication={"reason":"the user has changed",...}
without the clearing:  /main and /sub both carry it
```

So any page with an embedded controller, an ESI fragment or an error sub-request lost the message on the profile people actually open. `reset()` alone gives the right lifetime, since `services_resetter` runs before each main request, so the clearing is gone and the comment with it.

`testDeauthenticationIsNotRepeatedInSubRequestProfiles` asserted that behavior, so it is replaced by `testDeauthenticationReachesEveryProfileOfTheRequest`. Worth noting it passed while the real ordering was inverted, because it called `collect()` twice without any notion of which profile was which.

Two coverage holes, both found by mutating rather than reading. Deleting `addCompilerPass(new RemoveSecurityDataCollectorListenerPass())` from `SecurityBundle::build()` left the whole suite green, so the blocker you had just fixed had no guard; `SecurityBundleTest` covers the registration now. And `$userChangedBy[] =` could be replaced by `$userChangedBy = [...]` with nothing failing, so `testDeauthenticatedEventAggregatesEveryProviderThatSawAChange` pins the multi-provider case @MatTheCat asked about. Both fail when the fix is reverted.

Last, "de-authenticated" in the template is now "deauthenticated", matching the rest of the codebase including this commit's own changelog entries.
